      "math_academy_id": "q-16216",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/1ae30d99b72eaa6267e28951f87b77d0ed02b1a64d59efce03a9708090b00ca3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/d0c1765a4b35cbbfc8fdb4ee6182937d7abcd53aa779d63066f6b7937128bec3.png)",
